Still struggling a little bit with inserting waits in the battlelog, I found the
Victor Engine
had a free plugin to evaluate code in message windows, but my attempt with inserting
\e['this.push(\'wait\')']
in a battle message ended up just printing "['this.push('wait')']" at the end. Looking at the page again, it might just be intended for code that returns strings, or only compatible with regular message windows or something.
I was able to get some progress with the state messages specifically by digging into the plugin route, like so:
Code:
Window_BattleLog.prototype.displayCurrentState = function(subject) {
    var stateText = subject.mostImportantStateText();
    if (stateText) {
        this.push('addText', "\\c[\\v[6]]" + subject.name() + "\\c[0]" + stateText);
        this.push('wait');
        this.push('wait'); // extra wait
        this.push('clear');
    }
};
But it's a bit lacking in finesse. There's still the matter of battle messages for skills that don't have an animation, which extends to every skill that has a scope of "None", which all tend to have unique and important functions.
For getting the "No effect on X" message to go away on common event skills that need a specified target to work properly, I tried a similar plugin approach but wasn't getting any results attempting to add extra conditions to Window_BattleLog.prototype.displayFailure.  This might be because I was using the wrong call, the closest I could find to "the skill that is being evaluated right now" was
$gameTemp.lastActionData(0)
.
HOWEVER I found an extremely dumb workaround.
Adding the remove state trait for a state that the user cannot possibly have removes the failure message with 0 consequences on the effect, so I'm okay with calling that portion solved for now.

Not 100% sure what you're trying to do, but if you want a longer wait without pushing wait 2 times, just make a plugin to add a method like (and push 'longWait'):
Window_BattleLog.prototype.longWait = function() {
this._waitCount = this.messageSpeed() * 2;
};
Also, that workaround is the standard way, add/remove an empty dummy state. If you want to do it with code, I think it's something like:
Game_Action.prototype.itemEffectCommonEvent = function(target, effect) {
//
this.makeSuccess(target);
};
